1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
NALOXONE SAVES LIVES

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:06,000
No time to sit idly by. 

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,933
More and more people are dying of overdose from the likes of 

4
00:00:08,933 --> 00:00:12,666
heroin, fentanyl, and prescription pain medications

5
00:00:12,666 --> 00:00:14,966
like oxycodone and hydrocodone. 

6
00:00:14,966 --> 00:00:18,566
These are all examples of opioids.

7
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:21,633
Opioids are drugs derived from the opium poppy plant

8
00:00:21,633 --> 00:00:23,166
or made in the lab. 

9
00:00:23,166 --> 00:00:26,533
They can treat pain, cough and diarrhea.

10
00:00:26,533 --> 00:00:31,000
But opioids can also be addictive and even deadly. 

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,100
The number of opioid overdose deaths has escalated 

12
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:37,266
more than 400% since the turn of the century,

13
00:00:37,266 --> 00:00:41,500
with tens of thousands of lives now being lost every year.

14
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,800
But many deaths can be prevented with a life-saving treatment: naloxone.

15
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:48,333
When given right away, 

16
00:00:48,333 --> 00:00:52,000
naloxone can work in minutes to reverse an overdose. 

17
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,033
Naloxone is safe, has few side effects,

18
00:00:55,033 --> 00:00:59,566
and some forms can be administered by friends and family.

19
00:00:59,566 --> 00:01:02,033
When is naloxone used?

20
00:01:02,033 --> 00:01:06,400
You can save a life. First, recognize signs of overdose:

21
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:10,666
Limp body; Pale, clammy face; Blue fingernails or lips;

22
00:01:10,666 --> 00:01:15,200
Vomiting or gurgling sounds; Inability to speak or be awakened;

23
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,133
Slow breathing or heartbeat.

24
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:21,633
If you see these symptoms, call 911 immediately 

25
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:25,266
and consider the use of naloxone if available. 

26
00:01:25,266 --> 00:01:27,466
How is naloxone given?

27
00:01:27,466 --> 00:01:32,233
Home preparations include a nasal spray given to someone while they lie on their back 

28
00:01:32,233 --> 00:01:36,000
or a device that automatically injects medicine into the thigh. 

29
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,400
Sometimes more than one dose is needed.

30
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:42,200
The person’s breathing also needs to be monitored. 

31
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:44,300
If the person stops breathing, 

32
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:50,066
consider rescue breaths and CPR if you are trained until first responders arrive. 

33
00:01:50,066 --> 00:01:52,466
How does naloxone work?

34
00:01:52,466 --> 00:01:55,100
Naloxone is an opioid antagonist, 

35
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:58,800
which means that it blocks opioid receptors from being activated. 

36
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:04,000
It is so strongly attracted to the receptors that it knocks other opioids off. 

37
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,533
When opioids are sitting on their receptors,

38
00:02:06,533 --> 00:02:09,266
they change the activity of the cell.

39
00:02:09,266 --> 00:02:13,600
Opioid receptors are found on nerve cells all around the body:

40
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,100
In the brain, opioids produce feelings of comfort and sleepiness.

41
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:22,866
In the brainstem, opioids relax breathing and reduce cough.

42
00:02:22,866 --> 00:02:28,533
In the spinal cord and peripheral nerves, opioids slow down pain signals.

43
00:02:28,533 --> 00:02:33,433
In the gastrointestinal tract, opioids are constipating. 

44
00:02:33,433 --> 00:02:36,300
These opioid actions can be helpful! 

45
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:40,333
The body actually produces its own opioids called “endorphins,” 

46
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:43,066
which help calm the body in times of stress. 

47
00:02:43,066 --> 00:02:45,566
Endorphins help produce the “runner’s high” 

48
00:02:45,566 --> 00:02:48,566
that helps marathon runners get through grueling races.

49
00:02:48,566 --> 00:02:52,733
But opioid drugs, like prescription pain medications or heroin, 

50
00:02:52,733 --> 00:02:55,266
have much stronger opioid effects. 

51
00:02:55,266 --> 00:02:57,066
And they are more dangerous.

52
00:02:57,066 --> 00:03:01,800
Over time, frequent opioid use makes the body dependent on the drugs. 

53
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:04,000
When the opioids are taken away, 

54
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,233
the body reacts with withdrawal symptoms such as headache, 

55
00:03:07,233 --> 00:03:13,033
racing heart, soaking sweats, vomiting, diarrhea, and tremors. 

56
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:17,733
For many, the symptoms feel unbearable.

57
00:03:17,733 --> 00:03:22,300
With continued use, opioid receptors also become less responsive 

58
00:03:22,300 --> 00:03:25,533
and the body develops tolerance to the drugs. 

59
00:03:25,533 --> 00:03:28,733
More drugs are needed to produce the same effects...

60
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:31,633
which makes overdose more likely.

61
00:03:31,633 --> 00:03:37,300
Overdose is dangerous especially for its effect in the brainstem, relaxing breathing. 

62
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:43,000
Breathing can be relaxed so much that it stops…leading to death.

63
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:47,533
Naloxone knocks opioids off their receptors all around the body. 

64
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:51,133
In the brainstem, naloxone can restore the drive to breathe. 

65
00:03:51,133 --> 00:03:52,733
And save a life. 

66
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:55,366
But even if naloxone is successful, 

67
00:03:55,366 --> 00:03:57,666
opioids are still floating around, 

68
00:03:57,666 --> 00:04:01,533
so expert medical care should be sought as soon as possible.

69
00:04:01,533 --> 00:04:07,366
Naloxone works for 30-90 minutes before the opioids return to their receptors. 

70
00:04:07,366 --> 00:04:12,766
Naloxone may promote withdrawal because it knocks opioids off their receptors so quickly.

71
00:04:12,766 --> 00:04:18,433
But otherwise naloxone is safe and unlikely to produce side effects. 

72
00:04:18,433 --> 00:04:20,733
Naloxone saves lives. 

73
00:04:20,733 --> 00:04:23,700
From 1996 to 2014, 

74
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:28,733
at least 26,500 opioid overdoses in the United States were reversed

75
00:04:28,733 --> 00:04:31,700
by laypersons using naloxone. 

76
00:04:31,700 --> 00:04:34,833
While naloxone is a potentially life-saving treatment, 

77
00:04:34,833 --> 00:04:38,933
more needs to be done to solve the opioid overdose epidemic. 

78
00:04:38,933 --> 00:04:43,733
The National Institutes of Health launched the HEAL Initiative in 2018, 

79
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:47,800
expanding research across multiple NIH Institutes and Centers

80
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:52,000
to speed scientific solutions to the national opioid crisis. 

81
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:56,500
Research is underway to improve treatments for opioid misuse and addiction, 

82
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:58,733
and to enhance pain management. 

83
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:01,533
The National Institute on Drug Abuse, or NIDA, 

84
00:05:01,533 --> 00:05:06,833
is the leading NIH institute for research on opioid misuse and addiction, 

85
00:05:06,833 --> 00:05:12,000
and its support helped the development of the user-friendly naloxone nasal spray.

86
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,533
For more information, see NIDA’s website at drugabuse.gov and search “naloxone,” 

87
00:05:17,533 --> 00:05:22,566
or visit nih.gov and search “NIH heal initiative." 

88
00:05:22,566 --> 00:05:28,000
General opioid information can also be found at MedlinePlus.gov.

89
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,133
This video was produced by MedlinePlus, 

90
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:36,000
a trusted source of health information from the National Library of Medicine. 
